\chapter{Modules, imports, and exports}
\label{ch:31}
\index{Modules, imports, and exports}

A source import binds either a qualified namespace or selected names in the importing file. \OnxCodeTerm{import std.Int as Int;} provides qualified access. \OnxCodeTerm{from std import String, List;} introduces selected names. Relative \OnxCodeTerm{from .child import item;} follows the file's package directory. Package initializers live in \OnxCodeTerm{\_\_init\_\_.on1x}. Aliases change how the importing unit refers to declarations while preserving their implementation identity.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none]
import std.Int as Int;
from std import String;

fn main()
{
  assert(String.contains("On1x modules", "modules"));
  println(Int.div(-7, 3));
}
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

Distinguish explicit exports and re-exports from internal helpers when designing a library. For a package example, have the initializer re-export a single function from a sibling and use that public name from a separate program. Then exercise an unavailable name and see where lookup fails. Inspect \OnxCodeTerm{--deps} if the right name resolves to the wrong file. The qualified spelling needs adjacent dots, as discussed in \cref{ch:6}.

